+# ai.desertant:title
+
+Titles and descriptions for Android: a short factual title and a one- or two-sentence description for any passage of text (a voice-note transcript, a note, an email), written on device in the passage's language. The full model page is [docs/models/title.md](../../docs/models/title.md).
+
+The AAR carries one Swift JNI library per ABI (`libTitleAndroid.so`, arm64-v8a and x86_64), which includes `CTitleEngine`, the C decoder that runs the published MLX files. It depends on `ai.desertant:core` for the shared host bridge and LiteRT, so use it with the same version of core as every other `ai.desertant:*` model in the app.
+
+## Usage
+
+```kotlin
+import ai.desertant.title.Title
+
+val title = Title(context)                    // or Title(context, "/path/to/model/dir")
+val card = title.describe(noteText)           // suspend; runs off the main thread
+println("${card.title}: ${card.description}")
+title.close()
+```
+
+- `Title(context: Context, directory: String? = null)`: construction is cheap. The model loads on the first `describe` or on `download()`. A `directory` that already holds the model files is used offline as-is.
+- `fun isDownloaded(): Boolean`: whether the model is usable with no network.
+- `suspend fun download()`: fetch and verify the pinned revision (about 290 MB) into the app cache or `directory`. A no-op once present.
+- `suspend fun describe(text: String): Card`: greedy decoding, at most 96 tokens. Blank text returns an empty `Card` without running the model. `Card.isEmpty` is the only failure signal from the model; a native failure throws `TitleException`.
+- `close()`: release the native model.
+
+## Model files
+
+`model.safetensors`, `model.safetensors.index.json`, `config.json`, `generation_config.json`, `tokenizer.json`, `tokenizer_config.json` and `chat_template.jinja` from `desert-ant-labs/title` at the pinned revision: the same MLX folder Apple platforms load.
+
+## Long passages
+
+A passage longer than 1024 tokens keeps its first 768 and last 256 tokens, joined by `[...]`, so the time to the first token stays bounded on a phone (about 1100 prompt tokens at most).
